1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,820
Let me guess how you picture a retirement.

2
00:00:02,400 --> 00:00:05,500
There's a beach, maybe a sunset, definitely a cruise ship.

3
00:00:05,940 --> 00:00:10,180
You've got a golf club in one hand, a drink with a little umbrella in the other, and grandkids

4
00:00:10,180 --> 00:00:13,060
laughing around a Christmas tree and perfect matching sweaters.

5
00:00:13,620 --> 00:00:17,840
It's the highlight reel, the postcard, the version they put in the brochure for the retirement

6
00:00:17,840 --> 00:00:20,380
community with the suspiciously green lawns.

7
00:00:20,740 --> 00:00:22,720
The thing is, that stuff is real.

8
00:00:23,180 --> 00:00:25,500
Those moments do happen, but that's not retirement.

9
00:00:26,140 --> 00:00:27,480
That's maybe 5% of retirement.

10
00:00:27,480 --> 00:00:29,580
That's the trailer, not the movie.

11
00:00:30,200 --> 00:00:33,500
Because you want to know what retirement actually is, it's a regular Tuesday.

12
00:00:34,160 --> 00:00:38,220
It's a random, unremarkable Tuesday in the middle of February when there's no cruise,

13
00:00:38,640 --> 00:00:40,360
no grandkids, no umbrella drink.

14
00:00:40,820 --> 00:00:44,940
It's just you, your house, your coffee, and a whole lot of hours you get to fill however

15
00:00:44,940 --> 00:00:45,560
you want.

16
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:48,320
And you're going to have about 10,000 of those Tuesdays.

17
00:00:48,920 --> 00:00:53,820
So, here's the uncomfortable truth I've figured out watching people do this well and watching

18
00:00:53,820 --> 00:00:54,840
people do this badly.

19
00:00:55,340 --> 00:00:59,360
Whether you thrive in retirement has almost nothing to do with the big trips.

20
00:00:59,900 --> 00:01:01,640
It's decided on the ordinary Tuesday.

21
00:01:02,360 --> 00:01:05,940
It's decided by the small, quiet, daily stuff nobody posts about.

22
00:01:06,760 --> 00:01:11,860
The retirees who are genuinely deeply thriving, the ones you make you go, man, I want what

23
00:01:11,860 --> 00:01:12,380
they have.

24
00:01:12,800 --> 00:01:16,020
They all run a certain set of daily routines, little ones.

25
00:01:16,380 --> 00:01:18,560
And once you see them, you can't unsee them.

26
00:01:19,060 --> 00:01:20,820
So today, I'm handing you 8 of them.

27
00:01:21,080 --> 00:01:24,160
These are some of the daily routines of a thriving retiree.

28
00:01:24,160 --> 00:01:28,940
My goal is to make sure your retirement is worth waking up for, not just funded, but

29
00:01:28,940 --> 00:01:29,800
actually good.

30
00:01:30,320 --> 00:01:30,920
Let's get into it.

31
00:01:31,680 --> 00:01:33,740
Routine number one, they chase the daylight.

32
00:01:34,660 --> 00:01:38,460
And I know that sounds almost too simple to matter, but stick with me because this one

33
00:01:38,460 --> 00:01:39,880
punches way above its weight.

34
00:01:40,580 --> 00:01:42,200
Here's what happens to a lot of folks in retirement.

35
00:01:42,820 --> 00:01:46,100
Slowly, without ever deciding to, they become indoor creatures.

36
00:01:46,780 --> 00:01:51,960
The world shrinks down to the couch, the kitchen, the TV, and the roughly 11 feet of carpet between

37
00:01:51,960 --> 00:01:52,160
them.

38
00:01:52,720 --> 00:01:56,760
Days go by, where the closest they get to nature is watching a documentary about it.

39
00:01:57,260 --> 00:01:59,160
And your body absolutely hates this.

40
00:01:59,740 --> 00:02:03,900
You are running on hardware that was built over hundreds of thousands of years to be outside

41
00:02:03,900 --> 00:02:04,760
in the light.

42
00:02:05,380 --> 00:02:08,140
Morning Sunshine is what tells your brain what time it is.

43
00:02:08,480 --> 00:02:13,180
It sets your internal clock, which is the thing that decides whether you sleep like a baby or

44
00:02:13,180 --> 00:02:16,620
lie awake at 3 a.m. rearranging your entire life in your head.

45
00:02:17,300 --> 00:02:20,220
Get the light early and everything downstream gets easier.

46
00:02:20,220 --> 00:02:25,200
Your mood lifts, your energy shows up, your sleep that night gets better before you've

47
00:02:25,200 --> 00:02:25,940
even gotten tired.

48
00:02:26,660 --> 00:02:29,860
And you don't have to go conquer a mountain, I'm not sending you on an expedition.

49
00:02:30,600 --> 00:02:35,220
I'm talking 10 minutes on the porch with your coffee, a slow loop around the block, sitting

50
00:02:35,220 --> 00:02:40,440
in the backyard doing absolutely nothing but existing in the sun like a very content, very

51
00:02:40,440 --> 00:02:41,260
wise lizard.

52
00:02:41,800 --> 00:02:42,540
Chase the daylight.

53
00:02:43,220 --> 00:02:47,180
It's free, it's everywhere, and your whole system is quietly begging for it.

54
00:02:47,860 --> 00:02:50,560
Routine number two, they treat sleep like it's sacred.

55
00:02:51,380 --> 00:02:55,840
Now there's this weird backwards belief floating around that sleep is for lazy people, that

56
00:02:55,840 --> 00:03:00,700
real go betters power through on five hours, that needing a full night's rest is somehow

57
00:03:00,700 --> 00:03:01,580
a character flaw.

58
00:03:02,220 --> 00:03:03,660
Here's what most people don't realize.

59
00:03:04,420 --> 00:03:07,820
Sleep is when your body does maintenance, it's the overnight repair crew.

60
00:03:08,200 --> 00:03:12,660
While you're out, your brain is literally power washing itself, clearing out the gunk

61
00:03:12,660 --> 00:03:13,780
that builds up during the day.

62
00:03:13,780 --> 00:03:18,660
And skimping on that, night after night, year after year is genuinely one of the worst

63
00:03:18,660 --> 00:03:21,080
things you can do to an aging brain and body.

64
00:03:21,900 --> 00:03:23,500
But here's the trap specific to retirement.

65
00:03:24,440 --> 00:03:26,760
When you were working, your sleep had guardrails.

66
00:03:27,080 --> 00:03:30,200
You had to be up at a certain time, so you had to be down at a certain time.

67
00:03:30,780 --> 00:03:32,600
Retirement rips those guardrails out.

68
00:03:33,080 --> 00:03:36,140
And suddenly it's, eh, I'll stay up till two, I can nap tomorrow.

69
00:03:36,660 --> 00:03:39,960
And three weeks later, your sleep schedule looks like a toddler's finger painting.

70
00:03:40,360 --> 00:03:42,080
No pattern, no rhythm, just chaos.

71
00:03:42,080 --> 00:03:47,980
The thriving retirees keep the rhythm, roughly the same bedtime, roughly the same wake up,

72
00:03:48,400 --> 00:03:50,060
a dark, cool, quiet room.

73
00:03:50,500 --> 00:03:54,500
And critically, they get the screen out of the bedroom because scrolling in bed at midnight

74
00:03:54,500 --> 00:03:57,260
is like drinking espresso and wondering why you can't sleep.

75
00:03:57,960 --> 00:03:58,780
Guard your sleep.

76
00:03:59,420 --> 00:04:02,180
Every other routine on this list works better when you're rested.

77
00:04:02,680 --> 00:04:04,760
It's the foundation the whole house sits on.

78
00:04:05,040 --> 00:04:08,320
Routine number three, they go on an information diet.

79
00:04:09,170 --> 00:04:13,310
Let me describe a very common retirement day and tell me if it sounds familiar.

80
00:04:14,170 --> 00:04:15,850
You wake up, you turn on the news.

81
00:04:16,410 --> 00:04:16,710
It's outrage.

82
00:04:17,450 --> 00:04:18,110
You check your phone.

83
00:04:18,530 --> 00:04:19,130
It's more outrage.

84
00:04:19,750 --> 00:04:22,610
Plus, somebody you went to high school with is very mad about something.

85
00:04:23,270 --> 00:04:27,050
You've got the news channel running in the background all afternoon feeding you a steady

86
00:04:27,050 --> 00:04:29,870
drip of everything as a disaster and it's all getting worse.

87
00:04:30,850 --> 00:04:35,330
And then you wonder why by dinnertime you feel anxious, angry, and vaguely doomed even

88
00:04:35,330 --> 00:04:38,390
though nothing bad actually happened to you personally all day.

89
00:04:39,150 --> 00:04:40,270
That's not you getting older.

90
00:04:40,690 --> 00:04:41,790
That's you getting marinated.

91
00:04:42,310 --> 00:04:42,750
Here's the deal.

92
00:04:43,350 --> 00:04:46,410
When you were working, you didn't have time to soak in this stuff all day.

93
00:04:46,610 --> 00:04:47,830
Now you've got the time.

94
00:04:48,310 --> 00:04:51,450
And there is an entire industry, a very profitable one,

95
00:04:51,550 --> 00:04:56,190
built specifically to grab your attention and keep it by keeping you scared and angry.

96
00:04:56,650 --> 00:04:58,350
They are extremely good at their job.

97
00:04:58,710 --> 00:05:00,110
They are not on your side.

98
00:05:00,450 --> 00:05:03,370
And I'm not telling you to stick your head in the sand and ignore the world.

99
00:05:03,370 --> 00:05:04,310
Stay informed.

100
00:05:04,790 --> 00:05:05,810
That's part of being a grown up.

101
00:05:06,330 --> 00:05:09,250
But thriving retirees are ruthless about the dose.

102
00:05:09,770 --> 00:05:12,850
They check the news once, on purpose, and then they shut it off.

103
00:05:13,270 --> 00:05:17,110
They don't leave the outrage machine running all day like background music for their brain.

104
00:05:17,910 --> 00:05:18,930
Because here's the punchline.

105
00:05:19,450 --> 00:05:21,510
You spent 40 years earning this piece.

106
00:05:22,010 --> 00:05:26,050
Do not hand it back for free to a screen that's monetizing your blood pressure.

107
00:05:26,770 --> 00:05:29,730
Control the information or the information controls you.

108
00:05:30,450 --> 00:05:31,370
Routine number four.

109
00:05:31,890 --> 00:05:33,110
They tend to something living.

110
00:05:34,260 --> 00:05:37,760
And this one sneaks up on people because it doesn't sound like a happiness strategy.

111
00:05:38,440 --> 00:05:41,700
But there is something deep in the human wiring that needs to be needed.

112
00:05:42,200 --> 00:05:44,860
For most of your life, that need got filled automatically.

113
00:05:45,520 --> 00:05:46,980
You have a job that depended on you.

114
00:05:47,280 --> 00:05:48,420
Maybe kids who needed raising.

115
00:05:48,900 --> 00:05:52,120
A whole ecosystem of people and responsibilities pulling on your sleeve.

116
00:05:53,180 --> 00:05:54,040
Then retirement arrives.

117
00:05:54,720 --> 00:05:57,060
And a lot of that just stops needing you.

118
00:05:57,060 --> 00:06:01,920
And that can leave a quiet, hard to name whole where being counted on used to live.

119
00:06:02,740 --> 00:06:07,180
So the thriving retirees fill it on purpose with something that has to be tended.

120
00:06:07,840 --> 00:06:09,780
Tomato plants that'll die if you forget them.

121
00:06:10,060 --> 00:06:13,880
A dog that will absolutely stage a one animal protest if you skip the walk.

122
00:06:14,420 --> 00:06:16,160
A little herb garden on the windowsill.

123
00:06:16,560 --> 00:06:19,000
A cat who tolerates you barely on a good day.

124
00:06:19,700 --> 00:06:23,660
Something with a heartbeat or at least a root system that responds to your care.

125
00:06:24,420 --> 00:06:25,460
Because here's what that does.

126
00:06:25,940 --> 00:06:28,920
It gives every day a small non-negotiable.

127
00:06:29,280 --> 00:06:31,340
The plants don't care that you don't feel like it.

128
00:06:31,680 --> 00:06:33,080
The dog doesn't accept excuses.

129
00:06:33,820 --> 00:06:37,500
And that gentle little tug of something out there depends on me showing up.

130
00:06:37,900 --> 00:06:40,960
That turns out to be one of the most grounding forces in a human life.

131
00:06:41,740 --> 00:06:45,720
There's a reason nursing homes that give residents a plant or a pet to care for

132
00:06:45,720 --> 00:06:47,960
see people just light back up.

133
00:06:48,640 --> 00:06:49,580
Being needed is medicine.

134
00:06:50,340 --> 00:06:51,620
Go find something that needs you.

135
00:06:52,490 --> 00:06:53,410
Routine number five.

136
00:06:53,830 --> 00:06:55,530
They eat like they respect the chef.

137
00:06:56,490 --> 00:06:57,050
Here's the trap.

138
00:06:57,550 --> 00:06:59,050
And most people walks right into it.

139
00:06:59,550 --> 00:07:01,870
When your day had a shape, your meals had a shape.

140
00:07:02,170 --> 00:07:03,930
Breakfast happened because you were headed somewhere.

141
00:07:04,490 --> 00:07:07,010
Lunch was at lunchtime because that's the rule at the office.

142
00:07:07,410 --> 00:07:08,070
Dinner was dinner.

143
00:07:09,050 --> 00:07:10,670
Retirement dissolves all of that.

144
00:07:11,050 --> 00:07:13,510
And what fills the gap for a shocking number of people

145
00:07:13,510 --> 00:07:16,190
is what I lovingly called the sink brazes.

146
00:07:16,410 --> 00:07:19,830
That's when your breakfast is four crackers in the cold coffee from yesterday's pot.

147
00:07:19,830 --> 00:07:23,350
Lunch is a handful of whatever's in the pantry eaten standing up.

148
00:07:23,750 --> 00:07:27,770
And dinner is cereal because honestly, why dirty a whole pan for one person?

149
00:07:28,450 --> 00:07:29,570
Ben there, no judgment.

150
00:07:30,230 --> 00:07:32,830
But your body at this stage of life is keeping the receipts.

151
00:07:33,590 --> 00:07:34,090
Here's what changes.

152
00:07:34,870 --> 00:07:37,410
When you were 25, your body was a forgiving miracle.

153
00:07:37,810 --> 00:07:40,750
You could eat a gas station hot dog at midnight and bounce right back.

154
00:07:41,390 --> 00:07:42,870
That grace period is over.

155
00:07:43,550 --> 00:07:45,910
Now what you eat shows up that same afternoon.

156
00:07:46,370 --> 00:07:49,330
In your energy, your mood, your joints, how well you sleep.

157
00:07:49,330 --> 00:07:50,050
All of it.

158
00:07:50,680 --> 00:07:54,480
Food stops being background noise and becomes the single biggest lever you

159
00:07:54,480 --> 00:07:57,440
pull on how you feel twice a day, every day.

160
00:07:58,260 --> 00:08:00,420
And thriving retirees keep it dead simple.

161
00:08:00,860 --> 00:08:05,060
Real food, color on the plate, enough protein to keep the body strong.

162
00:08:05,900 --> 00:08:07,740
Actual water, not just coffee and hope.

163
00:08:08,380 --> 00:08:10,540
They're not on some grim, joyless diet.

164
00:08:10,940 --> 00:08:12,660
The dessert still shows up and it should.

165
00:08:12,980 --> 00:08:15,240
You've earned the pie, but the pie is the treat.

166
00:08:15,600 --> 00:08:16,440
It's not the food group.

167
00:08:17,240 --> 00:08:19,020
Feed yourself like you're worth the effort.

168
00:08:19,360 --> 00:08:22,000
Because you're stuck living in that body for a good long while.

169
00:08:22,440 --> 00:08:25,580
Routine number six, they keep their nest in order.

170
00:08:26,120 --> 00:08:27,020
And I want to be clear.

171
00:08:27,400 --> 00:08:32,300
I am not talking about becoming some obsessive neat freak who alphabetizes the spice rack

172
00:08:32,300 --> 00:08:34,540
and vacuums in perfectly straight lines.

173
00:08:35,020 --> 00:08:36,200
If that's you, great.

174
00:08:36,520 --> 00:08:37,320
But that's not the point.

175
00:08:37,720 --> 00:08:39,880
The point is way more powerful than tidiness.

176
00:08:40,680 --> 00:08:42,120
Here's the thing they don't tell you.

177
00:08:42,440 --> 00:08:46,440
Your outside space and your inside head space are basically roommates.

178
00:08:46,440 --> 00:08:52,200
When your outside environment is a chaotic mess, piles everywhere, clutter on every surface,

179
00:08:52,720 --> 00:08:57,920
that one chair that's now 90% laundry, your brain reads that as unfinished business.

180
00:08:58,340 --> 00:09:01,260
Low grade stress all day that you can't quite name.

181
00:09:01,980 --> 00:09:06,640
It's the mental equivalent of 40 tabs open in the background, quietly draining your battery.

182
00:09:07,340 --> 00:09:10,400
And retirement has a sneaky way of letting the mess creep in.

183
00:09:10,680 --> 00:09:14,800
When nobody's coming over and there's no rush, the little daily tidying slips.

184
00:09:14,800 --> 00:09:18,540
And one day you look up and the house feels like it's closing in on you.

185
00:09:19,020 --> 00:09:21,740
So the thriving retirees do a little bit daily.

186
00:09:22,200 --> 00:09:22,720
Make the bed.

187
00:09:23,200 --> 00:09:24,280
Do the one sink of dishes.

188
00:09:24,820 --> 00:09:26,500
Put the thing back where the thing goes.

189
00:09:27,060 --> 00:09:27,440
It's small.

190
00:09:27,940 --> 00:09:31,200
It takes 10 minutes and it does something bigger than a clean counter.

191
00:09:31,560 --> 00:09:35,380
It gives you a constant quiet stream of I'm on top of my life.

192
00:09:35,800 --> 00:09:38,220
Order out there creates calm in here.

193
00:09:38,680 --> 00:09:42,000
Plus, and I'll be honest with you, a made bed at 8 a.m.

194
00:09:42,000 --> 00:09:45,280
makes you feel like you've got your act together even if the rest of the day goes

195
00:09:45,280 --> 00:09:46,160
completely sideways.

196
00:09:46,920 --> 00:09:47,880
Never underestimate that.

197
00:09:48,280 --> 00:09:48,960
10 to your nest.

198
00:09:49,920 --> 00:09:52,300
Routine number seven, they laugh on purpose.

199
00:09:53,100 --> 00:09:57,300
Somewhere along the line, our culture decided that getting older means getting serious,

200
00:09:57,720 --> 00:10:00,980
getting stern, furrowed brow, grumbling at the TV,

201
00:10:01,340 --> 00:10:05,020
slowly transforming into the human version of a keep off the grass sign.

202
00:10:05,620 --> 00:10:08,280
And sure, some days the world earns a good grumble.

203
00:10:08,280 --> 00:10:11,700
But every genuinely happy retiree I have ever met

204
00:10:11,700 --> 00:10:15,140
has this one trait that jumps out at you within about 30 seconds.

205
00:10:15,580 --> 00:10:16,680
They laugh a lot.

206
00:10:17,020 --> 00:10:20,280
They've kept their sense of humor short and they point it at everything.

207
00:10:20,700 --> 00:10:23,900
The world, their aches, the news, and most of all themselves.

208
00:10:24,840 --> 00:10:26,580
And this isn't just feel good fluff.

209
00:10:27,100 --> 00:10:30,640
A real laugh, the kind where you can't catch your breath and your face hurts

210
00:10:30,640 --> 00:10:33,440
and you have to hold up a hand and gasp, stop, stop.

211
00:10:33,760 --> 00:10:36,640
That actually dumps stress right out of your body

212
00:10:36,640 --> 00:10:38,340
and does real good for your heart.

213
00:10:39,020 --> 00:10:39,780
But forget the science.

214
00:10:40,140 --> 00:10:44,320
You already know a great laugh is one of the best things a human being gets to feel

215
00:10:44,320 --> 00:10:46,800
and it costs exactly zero dollars.

216
00:10:47,260 --> 00:10:49,140
So the thriving ones go get it.

217
00:10:49,520 --> 00:10:50,720
They watch stuff that's funny.

218
00:10:51,060 --> 00:10:54,360
They tell the same terrible jokes they've been telling since the Nixon administration

219
00:10:54,960 --> 00:10:58,040
and they crack up at their own punchline before they even reach it.

220
00:10:58,520 --> 00:11:02,380
And the big one, they learn to laugh at aging instead of raging at it.

221
00:11:02,940 --> 00:11:06,480
Because you are going to walk into a room and completely forget why.

222
00:11:06,840 --> 00:11:08,020
That's just the arrangement now.

223
00:11:08,480 --> 00:11:13,000
You can be furious about it or you can laugh and turn around and stroll back in when it

224
00:11:13,000 --> 00:11:13,520
comes to you.

225
00:11:13,860 --> 00:11:15,560
One of those adds years to your life.

226
00:11:16,080 --> 00:11:16,940
Laugh on purpose.

227
00:11:17,400 --> 00:11:18,620
It's the best medicine there is.

228
00:11:19,260 --> 00:11:22,040
Routine number eight, they close the day with gratitude.

229
00:11:23,060 --> 00:11:24,820
Here's how most people end their day.

230
00:11:25,140 --> 00:11:26,800
Screen until the eyes give out.

231
00:11:27,280 --> 00:11:31,460
Fall asleep with the TV droning and the very last thoughts rattling around the skull

232
00:11:31,460 --> 00:11:35,040
are whatever the news scared them with or whatever they're dreading tomorrow.

233
00:11:35,640 --> 00:11:38,500
That's the note the whole day ends on, static and worry.

234
00:11:39,100 --> 00:11:41,180
And then they wonder why they wake up feeling behind.

235
00:11:41,900 --> 00:11:44,380
The thriving retirees ended completely differently.

236
00:11:44,920 --> 00:11:45,640
They take two minutes.

237
00:11:45,960 --> 00:11:46,880
That's it, two minutes.

238
00:11:47,320 --> 00:11:51,420
And they look back over the day and they deliberately go hunting for what was good,

239
00:11:51,780 --> 00:11:53,840
what went right, what they're thankful for.

240
00:11:54,400 --> 00:11:56,020
I know, I know, it sounds soft.

241
00:11:56,480 --> 00:11:59,660
But this is one of the most powerful two minute routines in existence.

242
00:11:59,660 --> 00:12:01,480
And here's the mechanics of why.

243
00:12:02,200 --> 00:12:05,400
Your brain came with a factory setting that clings to the bad stuff

244
